---
import BlogPostLayout from '../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title="Sample post 1 — plain prose"
  date="2026-05-08"
  readingTime="3 min read"
  description="The simplest possible blog post: title, date, prose. Use this as the starting template for most writing."
  tags={["template", "writing"]}
>
  <p>
    This is the simplest blog template — just title, date, and prose. Most posts
    only need this. Use it for short essays, reading notes, conference takeaways, or
    anything that's mostly continuous text.
  </p>

  <p>
    Paragraphs flow. Inline links look like <a href="https://www.dnotitia.com/">this</a>,
    and <strong>strong text</strong> reads with the heavier weight reserved for
    emphasis. <em>Italic</em> for titles, terms-of-art, or restrained accent.
  </p>

  <h2>A second-level heading</h2>

  <p>
    The first heading after the hero is an <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code>. Resist the urge
    to drop straight into <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>; readers parse hierarchy by
    counting steps from the title.
  </p>

  <p>
    Lists work too:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>One item per line.</li>
    <li>Keep them parallel — same grammatical form throughout.</li>
    <li>If an item needs more than two sentences, it's probably a paragraph.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>A subsection</h3>

  <p>
    Subsections (<code>h3</code>) for the second tier of structure. Don't go deeper
    than <code>h3</code> on a personal blog — if you need <code>h4</code>, the post
    is probably two posts.
  </p>

  <h2>Closing thought</h2>

  <p>
    End with a single concrete claim or a question. The reader should be able to
    state, in one sentence, what the post argued. If you can't, neither can they.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
